<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Aures</h1><p class="st-meta">484 CE – 703 CE (approx.) · capital Arris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Berber mountain kingdom in the Aurès that defied Vandals and Byzantines and ended with Queen Dihya's resistance to the Arabs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> mountains broke away from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom</a> around 484. An inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arris">Arris</a> records a ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masties">Masties</a>, who called himself duke for 67 years and emperor for ten, and claimed never to have broken faith with either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> or Moors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom drew on Roman titles and Christian faith while resting on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> clans of the mountains. In the 530s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iaudas">Iaudas</a> (Iabdas) raided Byzantine <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> and resisted the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, who finally stormed his strongholds in 539. Little is known of the following century. In the 690s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarawa-berbers">Jerawa</a> tribal leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Dihya</a>, called al-Kahina by Arab writers, led Berber resistance and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-al-numan">Hassan ibn al-Numan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hassan returned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Dihya</a> was killed around 701 to 703. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> absorbed the region, and many of her followers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and joined the Arab armies. Dihya became a lasting symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> resistance.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-aures.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Aures" width="320" height="223"><figcaption>Image: Ichthyovenator · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_the_Aures.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>484 CE – 703 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Masties, Iaudas, Dihya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Berber religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Algeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aurès kingdom, Kingdom of Iabdas, Kingdom of the Kahina</dd></dl></aside></div>
